WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:14.253
[This is an improvised talk (and intro)

00:00:14.277 --> 00:00:16.393
based on a suggested topic
from the audience.

00:00:16.417 --> 00:00:19.167
The speaker doesn't know
the content of the slides.]

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:21.768
Moderator: Our next speaker --

00:00:21.792 --> 00:00:25.417
(Laughter)

00:00:27.042 --> 00:00:28.292
is an --

00:00:30.625 --> 00:00:31.917
incredibly --

00:00:34.958 --> 00:00:36.559
(Laughter)

00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:39.684
Is an incredibly experienced linguist

00:00:39.708 --> 00:00:43.476
working at a lab at MIT
with a small group of researchers,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.893
and through studying our language

00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:48.309
and the way that we communicate
with other people,

00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:52.101
he has stumbled upon
the secret of human intimacy.

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:55.018
Here to give us his perspective,
please welcome to the stage,

00:00:55.042 --> 00:00:56.351
Anthony Veneziale.

00:00:56.375 --> 00:00:59.083
(Applause)

00:01:05.375 --> 00:01:09.042
(Laughter)

00:01:10.917 --> 00:01:14.643
Anthony Veneziale: You might think
I know what you're going through.

00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:17.434
You might be looking at me
here on the red dot,

00:01:17.458 --> 00:01:20.726
or you might be looking
at me on the screen.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.518
There's a one sixth of a second delay.

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:27.559
Did I catch myself? I did.

00:01:27.583 --> 00:01:30.934
I could see myself before I turned,

00:01:30.958 --> 00:01:35.018
and that small delay
creates a little bit of a divide.

00:01:35.042 --> 00:01:38.125
(Laughter)

00:01:39.792 --> 00:01:44.434
And a divide is exactly what happens
with human language,

00:01:44.458 --> 00:01:47.934
and the processing of that language.

00:01:47.958 --> 00:01:52.184
I of course am working
out of a small lab at MIT.

00:01:52.208 --> 00:01:54.809
(Laughter)

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:58.184
And we are scraping
for every insight that we can get.

00:01:58.208 --> 00:01:59.268
(Laughter)

00:01:59.292 --> 00:02:04.351
This is not often associated
with a computational challenge,

00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:08.726
but in this case,
we found that persistence of vision

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.101
and auditory intake

00:02:11.125 --> 00:02:14.976
actually have more in common
than we ever realized,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.518
and we can see it in this first slide.

00:02:17.542 --> 00:02:21.684
(Laughter)

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:25.851
(Applause)

00:02:25.875 --> 00:02:29.893
Immediately your processing goes to,
"Is that a hard-boiled egg?"

00:02:29.917 --> 00:02:31.851
(Laughter)

00:02:31.875 --> 00:02:36.059
"Is that perhaps the structural
integrity of the egg

00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:39.268
being able to sustain
the weight of what seems to be a rock?

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:41.917
Aha, is it in fact a real rock?"

00:02:43.208 --> 00:02:48.851
We go to questions
when we see visual information.

00:02:48.875 --> 00:02:52.458
But when we hear information,
this is what happens.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.559
(Laughter)

00:02:56.583 --> 00:03:01.101
The floodgates in our mind
open much like the streets of Shanghai.

00:03:01.125 --> 00:03:05.559
(Applause)

00:03:05.583 --> 00:03:08.184
So many pieces of information to process,

00:03:08.208 --> 00:03:11.976
so many ideas, concepts, feelings
and, of course, vulnerabilities

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.351
that we don't often wish to share.

00:03:14.375 --> 00:03:15.976
And so we hide,

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.476
and we hide behind what we like to call
the floodgate of intimacy.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.893
(Laughter)

00:03:22.917 --> 00:03:25.559
And what might that floodgate be holding?

00:03:25.583 --> 00:03:28.893
What is the dike upon which it is built?

00:03:28.917 --> 00:03:30.893
Well, first off --

00:03:30.917 --> 00:03:36.268
(Laughter)

00:03:36.292 --> 00:03:40.476
we found that it's different
for six different genotypes.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.833
(Applause)

00:03:49.958 --> 00:03:52.809
And, of course, we can start
categorizing these genotypes

00:03:52.833 --> 00:03:56.976
into a neuronormative experience
and a neurodiverse experience.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.101
(Laughter)

00:03:59.125 --> 00:04:00.976
On the right-hand side of the screen,

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.309
you're seeing spikes
for the neurodiverse thinking.

00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:06.726
Now, there are generally
only two emotional states

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:12.393
that a neurodiverse brain can tabulate
and keep count of at any given time,

00:04:12.417 --> 00:04:18.226
thereby eliminating the possibility
for them to be emotionally, sometimes,

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.518
attuned to the present situation.

00:04:20.542 --> 00:04:24.059
But on the left-hand side,
you can see the neuronormative brain,

00:04:24.083 --> 00:04:27.309
which can often handle
about five different pieces

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:30.684
of emotional cognitive information
at any given time.

00:04:30.708 --> 00:04:33.517
These are the slight variances
that you are seeing

00:04:33.541 --> 00:04:35.976
in the 75, 90 and 60 percentile,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.059
and then of course
that dramatic difference

00:04:38.083 --> 00:04:40.268
of the 25, 40 and 35 percentile.

00:04:40.292 --> 00:04:41.309
(Laughter)

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:43.643
But of course, what is the neural network

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:49.208
that is helping to bridge and build
these different discrepancies?

00:04:50.333 --> 00:04:53.292
(Laughter)

00:04:59.125 --> 00:05:00.393
Fear.

00:05:00.417 --> 00:05:02.268
(Laughter)

00:05:02.292 --> 00:05:04.542
(Applause)

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:11.018
And as we all know,
fear resides in the amygdala,

00:05:11.042 --> 00:05:13.309
and it is a very natural response,

00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:17.809
and it is very closely linked
with visual perception.

00:05:17.833 --> 00:05:21.018
It is not as closely linked
with verbal perception,

00:05:21.042 --> 00:05:24.018
so our fear receptors
often will be going off

00:05:24.042 --> 00:05:30.184
in advance of any of our cognitive usage
around verbal and words

00:05:30.208 --> 00:05:32.393
and cues of language.

00:05:32.417 --> 00:05:35.226
So as we see these fear moments,

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:38.018
we of course are taken aback.

00:05:38.042 --> 00:05:40.542
We stumble in a certain direction,

00:05:42.292 --> 00:05:45.184
generally away from the intimacy.

00:05:45.208 --> 00:05:47.143
(Laughter)

00:05:47.167 --> 00:05:50.518
Now of course, there's a difference
between the male perception

00:05:50.542 --> 00:05:52.018
and the female perception

00:05:52.042 --> 00:05:55.976
and of trans and those who are in between,
all of those as well,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.143
and outside of the gender spectrum.

00:05:58.167 --> 00:06:01.518
(Laughter)

00:06:01.542 --> 00:06:05.268
But fear is the central
underlying underpinning

00:06:05.292 --> 00:06:07.726
of all of our response systems.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:11.226
Fight-or-flight is one of the earliest,

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:15.226
some say reptilian,
response to our environment.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:20.559
How can we disengage or unhook ourselves
from the horns of the amygdala?

00:06:20.583 --> 00:06:22.684
(Laughter)

00:06:22.708 --> 00:06:25.643
Well, I'd like to tell you
the secret right now.

00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:28.875
(Applause)

00:06:33.583 --> 00:06:36.059
This is all making
much, much too much sense.

00:06:36.083 --> 00:06:38.333
(Laughter)

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:43.351
The secret lies

00:06:43.375 --> 00:06:46.601
in turning our backs to one another,

00:06:46.625 --> 00:06:50.309
and I know that that sounds
absolutely like the opposite

00:06:50.333 --> 00:06:52.309
of what you were expecting,

00:06:52.333 --> 00:06:56.643
but when in a relationship
you turn your back to your partner

00:06:56.667 --> 00:06:59.393
and place your back upon their back --

00:06:59.417 --> 00:07:01.976
(Laughter)

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.101
you eliminate visual cues.

00:07:04.125 --> 00:07:05.309
(Laughter)

00:07:05.333 --> 00:07:07.917
(Applause)

00:07:10.292 --> 00:07:13.309
You are more readily available

00:07:13.333 --> 00:07:17.143
to failing first,

00:07:17.167 --> 00:07:18.833
and failing first --

00:07:19.708 --> 00:07:21.809
(Laughter)

00:07:21.833 --> 00:07:27.393
far outweighs the lengths we go to

00:07:27.417 --> 00:07:29.809
to appeal to others,

00:07:29.833 --> 00:07:32.059
to our partners and to ourselves.

00:07:32.083 --> 00:07:35.059
We spend billions and billions of dollars

00:07:35.083 --> 00:07:38.351
on clothing, on makeup,

00:07:38.375 --> 00:07:40.750
on the latest trend of glasses,

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:45.518
but what we don't spend money and time on

00:07:45.542 --> 00:07:48.434
is connecting with each other

00:07:48.458 --> 00:07:50.684
in a way that is truthful

00:07:50.708 --> 00:07:51.976
and honest

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.893
and stripped of those visual receptors.

00:07:54.917 --> 00:07:58.500
(Applause)

00:08:00.542 --> 00:08:02.934
(Laughter)

00:08:02.958 --> 00:08:04.684
It sounds hard, doesn't it?

00:08:04.708 --> 00:08:08.625
(Laughter)

00:08:13.917 --> 00:08:15.958
But we want to be aggressive about this.

00:08:17.375 --> 00:08:19.625
We don't want to just sit on the couch.

00:08:20.792 --> 00:08:22.684
As a historian said earlier today,

00:08:22.708 --> 00:08:27.976
it's important to get up
and circumvent sometimes that couch.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.601
And how can we do it?

00:08:29.625 --> 00:08:32.458
Well yes, ice is a big part of it.

00:08:34.792 --> 00:08:37.601
Insights, compassion and empathy:

00:08:37.625 --> 00:08:39.934
I, C, E.

00:08:39.958 --> 00:08:42.750
(Applause)

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.582
And when we start using this ice method,

00:08:56.458 --> 00:09:00.500
well, the possibilities become
much bigger than us.

00:09:01.542 --> 00:09:04.833
In fact, they become smaller than you.

00:09:06.583 --> 00:09:08.934
On a molecular level,

00:09:08.958 --> 00:09:11.976
I believe that that insight

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.809
is the unifying theme

00:09:13.833 --> 00:09:16.934
for every talk you have seen so far at TED

00:09:16.958 --> 00:09:20.226
and will continue as we of course embark

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:25.476
on this journey here on this tiny planet,

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:28.601
on the ledge, on the precipice,

00:09:28.625 --> 00:09:32.059
as we are seeing, yes,
death is inevitable.

00:09:32.083 --> 00:09:34.684
(Laughter)

00:09:34.708 --> 00:09:37.268
Will it meet all of us at the same time,

00:09:37.292 --> 00:09:39.726
I think, is the variable we are inquiring.

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.750
(Laughter)

00:09:46.375 --> 00:09:49.143
I think that timeline gets a bit longer

00:09:49.167 --> 00:09:50.643
when we use ice

00:09:50.667 --> 00:09:53.875
and when we rest our backs
upon one another

00:09:55.458 --> 00:09:57.059
and build together,

00:09:57.083 --> 00:09:59.101
leaving behind the fear

00:09:59.125 --> 00:10:00.792
and working towards --

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.833
(Laughter)

00:10:14.125 --> 00:10:15.726
they'll edit this part out --

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.708
(Laughter)

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:24.643
a ripened experience of love,

00:10:24.667 --> 00:10:26.143
compassion,

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:28.476
intimacy based on a truth

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:32.184
that you are sharing from your mind's eye

00:10:32.208 --> 00:10:36.476
and the heart that we all can touch,

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.226
tactilely feel,

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:42.684
have maybe potentially a mushy experience

00:10:42.708 --> 00:10:45.833
that we don't just throw out
because it is browned,

00:10:47.333 --> 00:10:53.476
but let us slice in half
the experience we have gathered,

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.351
let us seed what the heart, the core,

00:10:56.375 --> 00:10:59.101
the seed of that idea in each of us is,

00:10:59.125 --> 00:11:01.226
and let us share it back to back.

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:02.643
Thank you very much.

00:11:02.667 --> 00:11:07.083
(Applause)

